 that means consumers probably paid more  money than that for that product than  they should have  potency testing for cannabinoid products  occurs at different stages this is an  example of what's required by the state  of Oregon the raw material is tested for  potency extracts are tested for potency  and infused products such as edibles  beverages and lotions are tested for  potency there are several different ways  cannabinoid products can be tested for  potency one way is using thin layer  chromatography which is also called TLC  this is a very fast easy and inexpensive  way to test for cannabinoids but  unfortunately it's not quantitative  meaning it does not give you an exact  result it gives you an estimate if we  look at the diagram below I will explain  how this testing is done the sample is  prepared by dissolving it in a solution  that sample is dropped or spotted on a  and absorbent matrix as the sample  passes through that absorbent matrix the  different cannabinoids are separated a  dye is added to the absorbent matrix and  we can visualize or estimate how much  cannabinoids we have in that product now  you can see on the right-hand side of  this diagram that it's very difficult to  distinguish the intensity of a 20% THC  containing compound and a 10 percent THC  compound so therein lies our problem  with THC all right with TLC testing gas  chromatography can also be used to test  for cannabinoid content gas  chromatography gives us an idea of the  total cannabinoid content but it cannot  be used to detect the acidic  and the reason for that is the sample is  heated in the gas chromatography  injector and when the sample is heated  the acidic cannabinoids are converted  into the neutral form therefore we are  only able to detect the neutral  cannabinoids using gas chromatography or  GC a gold standard for testing for  cannabinoid potency is high pressure  liquid chromatography it's also called  high-performance liquid chromatography  an HPLC the sample