ovide any sort of degradation of  that that sample this is a chromatogram  of the control so no stresses were  applied to the sample and we see a nice  sharp CBD peak and we added acid to the  CBD sample or CBD teacher we found the  Festiva tea converted to delta 9 and  delta 8 THC so this may be the reason  that some of those products that were  tested  in the the Journal of the American  Medical Association study contain THC  because the sample was not stable but  the product is not stable over time but  we've talked about pre-analytical error  or error that can occur during sampling  now we're going to talk about analytical  error and this is Error that occurs  during sample testing we need to think  about the materials that are used during  testing we need to think about the  methodology that is used during testing  we also need to think about instrument  and analyst accuracy now I mentioned  when I was talking about chromatograms  that typically we run a set of reference  standards and a reference standard is a  highly purified material that is used to  identify and quantify samples within  within a sample quantify compounds  within a sample in the pharmaceutical  industry reference standards are  typically kept as isolates or solids and  prepared in solution prior to sample  testing I mean the reason for that is  stability you don't want your reference  data to be degraded when you are testing  your samples so here in the United  States all cannabinoid isolates are  classified as schedule 1 controlled  substances which means that cannabis  testing laboratories must have a DEA  license in order to obtain cannabinoid  isolates and and many of these testing  laboratories do not so sanction DEA  exemptions are granted to manufacturers  of reference standard preparations  intended for laboratory use testing  laboratories rely upon the accuracy of  the CMA provided by the manufacturer for  these solutions so for some reason the  CFA did not accurately reflect the  concentration of the solution in the  ampule and the standard solution ampule  it would throw off all of the results  for the samples that they tested using  that solution so one thing I should  mention here is that since cannabinoids  our only soluble and alcohols most  reference stators or all reference  standards are typically prepared and  100% solvent we were curious to see how  accurate some of these reference  standards were in comparison to the CFA  so we were working with the laboratory  that does have a DEA license so they  were able to access cannabinoid  reference standards in the isolate or  solid form we injected the the standard  solutions for CBD that were prepared by  three manufacturers and we compared the  concentration result to the  concentration listed in the CMA for that  standard and we found that for the CBD  products or the CBD standards we tested  in all cases the concentration generated  in our laboratory was the same as what  was produced on the CMA but the case was  slightly different for Delta 9 THC we  found that one of those manufacturers  there see of aid didn't match the  concentration we were calculating in our  laboratory and the reason for that is if  you're analyzing are you performing  assay using two different  chromatographic methods it's possible  that one method will have a better  separation of the impurities from the  analyte of interest so the moral of the  story is it's important for cannabis  testing laboratories to to have an  incoming reference standard  qualification procedure and within this  procedure it's important to compare the  concentration on the CMA to the  concentration generated in their  laboratory to ensure that those two  things match with these reference data  materials it is important to handle the  solutions properly now these are highly  volatile solutions  they are prepared insolvent so the  solvent tends to evaporate and we found  that testing laboratories spend a lot of  money on these cannabinoid solutions but  the the they skimp on the materials so  they skimp on how they store these  solutions you can see in the photograph  here that the testing laboratory was  using a reference standard with a  punctured septum which means that that  reference standard was evaporating over  time if they had used a an HPLC vial  with a self-sealing septum it would have  kept that reference standard stable over  time so the moral of the story is don't  skimp on the quality of the materials  that are used after these these  expensive reference standards have been  purchased another observation that we've  made is that many testing laboratories  are using air displacement pipettes air  displacement pipettes are calibrated  with water and the cannabinoid reference  standards are prepared in solvent air  displacement pipettes work by producing  an airspace or using in a measured air  space between the top of the liquid  being pipetted and the piston of the  pipette now anytime we change from a an  aqueous solution or a pipetting of water  to a solvent solution we impact that  airspace we impact the calibration so we  found that it was very very difficult or  nearly impossible to produce a linear  curve or a linear standard curve  calibration curve using an air  displacement pipette the correct pipette  to use with these cannabinoid reference  standards would be a positive  displacement syringe pipette or a glass  pipette and this pipette eliminates that  airspace it eliminates the problems that  we see with  volatility of these solvent based  solutions and with the air displacement  pipette or glass pipette calibration  curves are quite linear and easier to  produce anytime we're working in  laboratory  we need to validate the chromatographic  methods that we're using a validation  ensures that the method works and it  works accurately in consistency there  are specific criteria for method  validation the method needs to be  specific for the material or compound of  interest that you're looking for it  needs to be linear and that means that  the method needs to be proportional to  the actual amount amount of the compound  present also the method needs to be  accurate so the difference between the  measured value and the real value needs  to be equal and also the method needs to  be precise meaning there's agreement  from multiple measurements of the same  sample and also the method needs to have  an accurate limit of detection so we  need to know how much or how low we can  go what's the lowest amount we can  detect with that method using using  chromatography so to reduce error it's  important to validate methods after any  changes in product formulation or  manufacturing of a product there are  several places we can go to get  information on method validation there  are some guidelines provided by the FDA  and there are also guidelines provided  by the International Committee on  harmonization and you see those two  documents here an example of a valid  method is a method that was recently  published in the journal as liquid  chromatography and related science  Sciences and in  method we were looking at the assay  determination for CB d there's been a  lot of talk lately regarding  standardized methods and standardized  methods are methods that have been  validated these are methods that have  been peer-reviewed and the results have  been compared between multiple labs  multiple instruments and multiple  analysts and standardized method by  definition is a method that's used  regularly by several labs over an  extended period of time  so these methods are tried-and-true  there's several years of experience  using the method and we know that they  work so where can you find a  standardized method well you can go to  the American herbal pharmacopoeia in the  American Herbal pharmacopoeia they have  an HPLC method for determination of  major phyto cannabinoids and cannabis it  gives you sample preparation procedures  there's one for crude cannabis there's  one for cannabis resin and cannabis oil  we see the ranges that can be tested  using this method we see the  chromatographic parameters and also the  calculations for that method are  provided so there are standardized  methods out there we just easy to go  where to look so why wouldn't you use a  standardized method well if you're using  if you have a formulation that causes an  interference and the chromatography for  the cannabinoids of interest you  probably want to create your own  chromatographic method you may want to  create your own method if you're looking  at impurities and you're trying to  identify contaminants also you may want  to create your own method if you are  investigating or trying to problem solve  some of those standardized methods may  not separate the peaks of interest that  you think that you want to look at so  feel free to develop your own separate  methods but make sure they work as  intended by validating those methods  whatever method you're using it is  important to perform a proficiency test  the Emerald test is one of the most  common proficiency tests and in this  test several laboratories are sent the  same sample and they test those samples  and send the information back to to the  Emerald company and the results are  statistically process for each  laboratory so the the laboratory can see  how accurate are they are how precise  they are compared to other laboratories  and this proficiency test is required by  some states for for laboratories to  maintain there are cannabis testing  accreditations you can also perform an  inter lab proficiency test so that's a  lab that's a test that that is done  within the laboratory so an inter lab  proficiency test would be using an  orthogonal method so you know Thugga no  method would be a method that uses  ultimate chromatographic selectivity  from the primary method some examples of  our THOG '''l methods would be methods  that operate at different PHS so if your  primary method performs a separation at  pH 10 you can have an orthogonal method  that does this that performs a  separation of each two same thing with  stationary phases you can change the  stationary phase and still perform  reverse phase chromatography and compare  the results you can also change the  chromatographic platform you may test  your sample by reverse phase you may  test your sample by SFC but as long as  you get the same result with those two  orthogonal methods you know that your  primary method is working properly we're  going to show you an example of how we  used an orthogonal method orthogonal  methodology we separated our reference  stand  using a pH 10 and we had an hemp extract  sample at one of our partnering  laboratories and when this happen  extract sample was tested we can see  based upon the retention time what our  cannabinoids are but we had one unknown  compound alluding in the chromatography  for the hemp extract we were able to  gain information by using in our thaw  Gadol method so we perform the  separation using the same column at pH 3  and we saw that the neutral cannabinoids  were not affected by the change in pH  but we found that the acidic  cannabinoids were affected by the change  in pH so we were able to gain  information on this unknown compound and  we know that it's likely an acidic  cannabinoid because it was affected by  the change in pH so essentially a  thought argA no methodology can help you  gain additional information on the  sample that you're testing there are so  four different ways to test cannabis  which I'm which I mentioned earlier in  the laboratory some some ways to test  cannabis are more accurate than others  you can use detection kits there are  also many portable devices on the  markets on the market and there's also  there are several HPLC instruments on  the market the most accurate way to test  your potency of canvas would be by HPLC  and that's because the variability is  very very low and not testing limits  also tried-and-true and used repeatedly  in the pharmaceutical environment if you  are a consumer and you're curious about  the products that you've purchased and  you want to get an idea of the  cannabinoid content then a detection kit  or a portable device is the best way to  go in the state testing regulations I  often see a regulation stating that  testing laboratories should have  qualified analyst  son staff so what is a qualified analyst  well it it means that the analyst has is  educated and it's also in in science or  in analytical testing but it also means  that that analyst has experience working  in a laboratory and the more experience  that analyst has working in a laboratory  the less likely that mistakes will  happen when I talk about mistakes in the  laboratory because we're all human  anything can happen  there are documentation errors that can  occur errors in technique calculation  errors and also we've seen contaminated  glassware being an issue and some  testing laboratories so the more  experience and training that an analyst  has the more likely the errors will be  caught and corrected early so this is  where we get to the fun stuff we've  talked about the pre-analytical error  which is the area that occurs in  sampling we've talked about analytical  error which occurs in laboratory and  we've also and now we're going to talk  about post analytical error and this is  where we get into result tempering and  lab shopping this this is probably the  worst error of them all a problem that  we've seen is that manufacturers know  that consumers tend to equate  cannabinoid product levels with price so  if a manufacturer produces a product  with very high cannabinoid levels they  will demand a higher price for it and  because of that there have been issues  with cutting and pasting on these  certificates of analysis we've also seen  lab shopping occur where a producer will  send out their sample to four different  testing laboratories and